course: dictionary
lesson: 15974
title: Unwrapped
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are a root word with a small change at the start or end. Unwrapped comes from the verb unwrap, which means to take the paper or cover off something. The -ed ending shows the action happened in the past, so «she unwrapped the present» means she already took the paper off.
explain: The word can also describe something that has no cover. «An unwrapped sweet» is a sweet with no paper on it. Read the words around it to see which meaning fits: a present being opened, or a sweet with no wrapper.
explain: Use the check: swap in your meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense. «She opened the present» works, so the first meaning fits. «A sweet with no paper» works for the second meaning too.
fact: Un- at the start can mean the opposite: wrap means to cover, and unwrap means to take the covering off.
fact: «Unwrapped» means opened by taking off the paper or cover.
fact: «Unwrapped» can also mean not wrapped, with no cover.
fact: Unwrapped sweets from strangers should not be eaten, because you cannot see how they were kept.
fact: Unwrapped food can get dirty, so it is kept covered.
exercise: «She unwrapped the present slowly.» What does unwrapped mean here? | *Opened by taking off the paper | Put inside a box | Lost under the bed | Covered again with new paper
exercise: Sam is offered «an unwrapped sweet» by a stranger in the park. What should Sam do? | Eat it quickly before anyone sees | Share it with the stranger | *Say no thank you and tell a trusted adult | Put it in a pocket to save for later
exercise: Why is food kept covered? | Covers make food taste sweeter | *Unwrapped food can get dirty | Covers make food heavier | Covered food is always cold
test: «Grandma unwrapped the gift at the festival.» What does unwrapped mean here? | Hidden in a drawer | Not wrapped at all | Thrown away | *Opened by taking off the paper
test: «The shop left the cake unwrapped on the counter.» What does unwrapped mean here? | *Not wrapped, with no cover | Lost | Frozen solid | Sold out
exam: What is the root word of unwrapped? | *Wrap | Unwrapping | Wrapped | Wrapper
